Excellent question 🔥 — this is the key to cracking any LLD round confidently, even when you’ve never seen the problem before.

Let’s build a repeatable 7-step framework used by top candidates in FAANG / FinTech / Product interviews 👇

🧭 7-Step Framework to Approach Any LLD Question
Step 1️⃣ — Clarify the Problem

Don’t jump to design — understand the intent.
Ask questions like:

Who are the users? (Actors)

What’s the main use case?

Are we building backend logic, or also UI/API?

What are in-scope and out-of-scope features?

📘 Example (Design a Parking Lot):

Clarify: Are we designing only backend logic? Should it support multiple floors, multiple vehicle types, and payment?

🎯 Goal: Know exactly what you need to build before touching design.

Step 2️⃣ — Identify Core Entities (Nouns)

Go through the problem statement and highlight the nouns — these are your classes or entities.

📘 Example (Payment Gateway):

Payment, Merchant, Transaction, PSP, LedgerEntry, Refund, Webhook.

Then identify relationships:

1-to-1 (e.g., Payment ↔ Transaction)

1-to-many (e.g., Merchant → Payments)

Aggregations (e.g., Payment includes multiple ledger entries)

🎯 Goal: Create a rough Entity list.

Step 3️⃣ — Identify Core Actions (Verbs)

Verbs often become methods or service-level operations.

📘 Example:

authorizePayment(), capturePayment(), refundPayment(), notifyMerchant()

🎯 Goal: Map verbs to entities or services — this forms your behavior layer.

Step 4️⃣ — Define System Boundaries (Modules/Services)

Now separate your design into logical layers:

Layer	Examples	Purpose
API Layer	Controllers, DTOs	Request handling
Service Layer	PaymentService, ParkingService	Business logic
Data Layer	Repositories, DAOs	Database access
Utility Layer	NotificationManager, Logger, Validators	Cross-cutting utilities

🎯 Goal: Keep SRP (Single Responsibility Principle) — each class should do one thing.

Step 5️⃣ — Design Class Diagram

Now map relationships:

Inheritance (is-a)

Composition (has-a)

Interfaces (polymorphism)

📘 Example:

interface PaymentMethod {
    boolean authorize();
    boolean capture();
}

class CardPayment implements PaymentMethod { ... }
class UpiPayment implements PaymentMethod { ... }


🎯 Goal: Capture key relationships + OOP principles (SOLID).

Step 6️⃣ — Define Interactions (Sequence Diagram)

For each major flow:

Identify actors and sequence.

Show which component talks to which (API → Service → Repository → External system).

📘 Example (Payment Authorization):

Merchant → PaymentController → PaymentService → PSPConnector → LedgerService → DB


🎯 Goal: Explain the flow of control clearly.
This is what most interviewers grade heavily.

Step 7️⃣ — Add Cross-Cutting Concerns

Once the design works, improve it:

Idempotency: Prevent duplicate requests.

Concurrency: Handle multiple users updating data.

Caching: Optimize performance.

Error Handling / Retries

Extensibility: Add new types easily (e.g., new PaymentMethod).

Scalability: Stateless services, async jobs, queues.

🎯 Goal: Show maturity in design — tradeoffs and flexibility.

🧩 Bonus Tips (That Impress Interviewers)

Talk in terms of evolution:
“I’ll first design a simple version, then make it extensible for multiple methods.”

Use Design Patterns naturally:

Strategy → for interchangeable behaviors (PaymentMethod)

Factory → for creating object types (VehicleFactory)

Observer → for notifications/webhooks

Singleton → for shared configs

State → for status transitions (Payment status, Order lifecycle)

Follow SOLID principles

Single Responsibility

Open/Closed (extensible for new types)

Liskov Substitution (interface-based)

Interface Segregation (small contracts)

Dependency Inversion (services depend on abstractions)

Use proper naming — avoid “Manager”, “Helper” unless justified.

Think in terms of data + behavior + interaction.

Data: Entities

Behavior: Methods

Interaction: Flow

